[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Hibernate Primary Key Generation Strategies"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right primary key generation strategy in Hibernate is crucial for application performance, database portability, and data integrity. Hibernate provides four main strategies through the @GeneratedValue annotation: Identity, Sequence, Table, and Auto."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Identity Strategy"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Identity strategy relies on an auto-incrementing database column. The database generates the primary key value upon insertion. While simple, it has a significant drawback: it disables JDBC batch inserts because Hibernate needs to retrieve the generated ID immediately after every insert statement."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private String username;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Sequence Strategy"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Sequence strategy uses a database sequence object to generate unique primary keys. This is the recommended approach for databases that support sequences (like PostgreSQL, Oracle, and MariaDB). It fully supports JDBC batching and high-performance inserts when combined with pooled optimizers."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "account_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "account_seq", sequenceName = "account_sequence", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
    private String accountNumber;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Table Strategy"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Table strategy uses a separate database table to persist and simulate primary key generation. This strategy offers maximum database portability since every database supports standard tables. However, it can cause performance bottlenecks and concurrency lock contention under high loads."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE, generator = "order_gen")
    @TableGenerator(name = "order_gen", table = "id_generator", pkColumnName = "gen_key", valueColumnName = "gen_value", allocationSize = 1)
    private Long id;
    private String orderNumber;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Auto Strategy"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Auto strategy delegates the choice of generation strategy to Hibernate. Hibernate selects Identity, Sequence, or Table based on the underlying database dialect's capabilities. In modern Hibernate versions (5.x and 6.x), Auto typically resolves to Sequence for databases that support them."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.AUTO)
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices Summary"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use GenerationType.SEQUENCE whenever your database supports sequences for optimal batching and performance."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid GenerationType.IDENTITY if you rely heavily on Hibernate batch processing (JDBC batch inserts)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid GenerationType.TABLE in high-throughput production environments due to table locking overhead."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always configure an allocationSize matching your sequence increment size (e.g., allocationSize = 50) to optimize database round-trips."}]
